WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.280
Speaker 1: I come to you today to tell you that&nbsp;
Chris Christie's polling is now up 150% since he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:18.120
announced he was running for president. However,&nbsp;
this puts him at two and a half. So, listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.700
percentage wise, things are going in the right&nbsp;
direction for Chris Christie. The clearly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.340
most sane and rational person running in the 2024&nbsp;
Republican primary. But is it going to be enough?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:35.760
I don't know. So let's take a look at some data.&nbsp;
Chris Christie announced that he was running on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.680
June 6th, but we learned that he was running on&nbsp;
May 31st, June 1st. So we're going to look at how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:46.380
polling has changed for the Republican candidates,&nbsp;
the top ones between June 1st and today. Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:53.880
for example, was at 53 when Christie announced,&nbsp;
and he is now at 52. So Trump is down 2% since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.940
Christie's announcement, DeSantis is totally&nbsp;
flat at absolutely flat, totally not woke at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:07.080
all. DeSantis was at 22 and he's still at 22. So&nbsp;
he has not seen any polling change. Mike Pence was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:17.400
at four but is now at six. Pence has gained 50%,&nbsp;
Nikki Haley unchanged four and four. Tim Scott was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:25.860
at 2%, Now polling 4%. He has gained 100% support,&nbsp;
doubling it. And then there is Chris Christie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:36.360
who was polling 1%, announced his candidacy&nbsp;
and is now polling 2.5%. That is a gain of 150%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:45.240
percent. Now, am I being serious? Well, I'm being&nbsp;
serious, but I'm also going to be honest with you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:52.440
about small numbers. Small numbers in general&nbsp;
can be inaccurate or meaningless in exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.960
these sorts of situations when you're looking&nbsp;
at changes because such small numbers can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:05.820
affected by rounding, they can be affected by data&nbsp;
truncation, they can be affected by inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:14.520
context, sample size. So for example, if someone&nbsp;
goes up from 1 to 2% in polling, it's technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:20.580
an increase of 100%. You've gone from 1 to 2.&nbsp;
It's mostly meaningless because these numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:26.520
are so small that they really may not reflect&nbsp;
the preferences of the population. And these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:34.020
small changes can easily be influenced by random&nbsp;
error measurement uncertainty. Sampling bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:39.420
etc.. So you do get rid of a little bit of this&nbsp;
by using averages of polls, which we are doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:46.980
Is Chris Christie gaining? I believe Chris&nbsp;
Christie is gaining. But to say that he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:55.380
really gained 150% support since his announcement&nbsp;
maybe is a bit further than we can go. What we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:02.520
are starting to get a picture of, and this is&nbsp;
really important, is that it may not be so simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:10.560
to significantly shift massive amounts of&nbsp;
support in the Republican primary. Trump's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.140
now been indicted twice. DeSantis went from&nbsp;
a potential candidate to a real candidate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:23.760
Trump's basically flat. DeSantis is basically&nbsp;
flat. Nobody else is polling any more than 6%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.840
So if there's any real takeaway right now, forget&nbsp;
about, oh, they were at one, now they're at three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.600
or whatever the case may be. The big takeaway&nbsp;
for me is that it is proving difficult to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:43.140
significant moves in Republican voters. The next&nbsp;
milestone, of course, becomes, well, number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.960
does Trump get indicted between July and September&nbsp;
in Georgia? And number two, in late August,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.400
the first Republican primary debate will take&nbsp;
place on Fox News. Bret BAIER will be hosting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.980
it. Does Trump show up to that debate and&nbsp;
does the debate performance actually shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:04.140
the numbers in any serious way? We have plenty&nbsp;
of time to think about that. This is where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.540
polling is today. We're going to take the quickest&nbsp;
break. Make sure you're subscribed on YouTube at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.820
YouTube.com, slash the David Paxman show. And&nbsp;
the show will simply continue in a moment.

